      "text": "For two lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be $-1$. To find the slopes, we start with the first equation:\n\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\nThe slope of this line is \\( m_1 = 2 \\).\n\nNext, we convert the second equation from the form \\( ax + by = c \\) to the form \\( y = mx + b \\):\n\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\nFirst, we isolate \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\nThen we divide both sides by 6:\n\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\n\nThe slope of this line is \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nSince the lines are perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\n\nSubstituting the slopes \\( m_1 = 2 \\) and \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\) into this equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1 \\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\n\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).\n\nLet's verify this by calculating the slopes and confirming that their product is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe first equation is:\n\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\nThe slope \\( m_1 = 2 \\).\n\nThe second equation is:\n\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\n\nThe slope \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nTo confirm, we check the product of the slopes:\n\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -\\frac{2a}{6} = -\\frac{a}{3} \\]\n\nSetting this equal to \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\n\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
