      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to compare their slopes. Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\nFirst, we rewrite the second line equation in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\).\n\nStarting with:\n\\[6y + ax = 6\\]\n\nWe isolate \\(y\\):\n\\[6y = -ax + 6\\]\n\\[y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\\]\n\nSo, the slope of the second line is \\(-\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\nNext, the slope of the first line is given by its equation:\n\\[y = 2x + 13\\]\n\nSo, the slope of the first line is \\(2\\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\):\n\\[-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\\]\n\\[-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\\]\n\\[a = 3\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[\\boxed{3}\\]",
