      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of the lines and use the condition that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe first line is given by the equation:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\nThis is in the slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. Therefore, the slope of the first line is:\n\\[ m_1 = 2 \\]\n\nThe second line is given by the equation:\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\nFirst, we need to rewrite this equation in the slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\). We do this by isolating \\( y \\):\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\nThis is now in the slope-intercept form, where the slope \\( m_2 \\) is:\n\\[ m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\]\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\nSubstituting the slopes we found:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\nTo solve for \\(a\\), we multiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
